% Where a host language's own rules were deciding the answer.
%
% Every case here separates a correct implementation from one that reached for
% its host's nearest built-in. They exist because a cross-host review found the
% SQL layer letting PHP's and Python's semantics choose the output -- and, in the
% worst of them, letting BOTH hosts choose the same wrong output, which no
% PHP-against-Python comparison can ever see.
%
% These run under every host that has a SQL layer, which is what makes them the
% enforcement mechanism: sql/mutations.json targets PHP and JSON, and
% sql/oracle/ is PHP-only, so a case file is the only witness the next four
% ports inherit automatically.

### name: neutral.key.leading-zero-is-not-a-position
--- note
SEL list keys are the canonical decimals "1", "2", … so "01" is not a key and
the evaluator answers E_NO_KEY. Both hosts answered *element 1*: PHP's
(int)"01" and Python's int("01") are both 1, and neither regex said no.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"column": "a"}, {"column": "b"}]}}
--- source
V["01"]
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING 1:2
===
### name: neutral.key.trailing-newline-is-not-a-position
--- note
The regex was `^[0-9]+$` in both hosts. PHP's `$` matches before a trailing
newline unless the D modifier is given, and Python's does too -- so "1\n"
selected element 1 in both. Dec::parse and decimal.py already carry the fix for
exactly this; the SQL layer had not inherited it. A full match is the rule.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"column": "a"}, {"column": "b"}]}}
--- source
V["1\n"]
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING 1:2
===
### name: neutral.key.canonical-position-still-resolves
--- note
The guard above must not cost the ordinary case.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"column": "a"}, {"column": "b"}]}}
--- source
V["2"]
--- expect
`b`
===
### name: neutral.key.leading-zero-over-a-static-list
--- note
The same key rule reached through an aggregate binder rather than a binding:
childOf() walks a `list` node and used the same host int parse.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ALL((1, 2), _K $== "1" AND _["01"] EQL 9)
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING 1:29
===
### name: neutral.key.leading-zero-is-not-a-relation-position
--- note
`rel[1]` is refused because rows have no order. "01" is not a position, so it
falls to the field lookup and is refused as a missing field -- which is what SEL
does with it. The two refusals must not be swapped by a lax regex.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "order_items", "alias": "oi",
           "fields": {"QTY": {"column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ANY(ITEMS, I, I["01"] > 0)
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING 1:16
===
### name: neutral.slot.leading-zero-is-not-a-slot
--- note
tools/gen-sql-map.mjs refused `{01}` and Map::define did not, so the shipped map
and a runtime-registered template were read by two grammars. Now one: canonical,
full-match, three digits.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "U({01})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: neutral.slot.trailing-newline-is-not-a-slot
--- note
JS reads `^[0-9]+$` strictly, so the generator always refused this; PHP did not.
The runtime grammar now matches the generator's.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "U({0\n})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: neutral.slot.canonical-slot-still-fills
--- note
And the ordinary registered template still works, including the {n:} rest form.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- register
[{"define": ["mariadb", "funcs", "UPPER", {"tpl": "U({0})", "ret": "TEXT"}]}]
--- source
UPPER("a")
--- expect
U('a')
